            "tags": [],
            "bug_id": 17845,
            "text": "Created attachment 2641\npatch diff to include dunn-bonferroni as method in p.adjust\n\nPapers by Olive Dunn from 1961 indicate that she was likely the first to truly apply Bonferroni corrections in practice.\n\n\nTherefore, I believe that the Bonferroni method should be modified to the Dunn-Bonferroni method (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dunn%E2%80%93Bonferroni_correction), and ideally the p.adjust code should be updated to reflect it.\n\n\nI have a patch where I've added a DB (for Dunn-Bonferroni) method, which just gets set internally to \"bonferroni\" internally, so that none of the underlying code needs to change.\n\n\nIn addition, the patch updates the p.adjust help page, and has added references based on the Wikipedia page. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dunn%E2%80%93Bonferroni_correction",
            "count": 0,
            "is_markdown": false,
            "creation_time": "2020-06-27T17:45:00Z",
            "creator": "rflight79"
          },
          {
            "tags": [],
            "is_private": false,
            "id": 104420,
            "creation_time": "2026-11-21T23:21:17Z",
            "count": 1,
            "text": "Created attachment 4140\nUpdated Patch adding Dunn-Bonferroni (\"DB\") to p.adjust.methods\n\nFrom a brief investigation, I'm seeing somewhat mixed signals with regard to whether 'Dunn-Bonferroni Method' is used as a general replacement for \"Bonferroni Method\", with some sources saying it is, while others seem to use Dunn-Bonferroni to refer to a subset or special case of Bonferroni. I tend to think that the former is ok, as Dunn's paper does seem to be widely considered seminal in the p-value adjustment literature.\n\nHow references are stored, tracked, and displayed in R documentation appears to have been completely revamped in the time since Robert submitted the initial patch, and as such it cannot be directly applied.\n\nI've attached an updated patch. AI was used for patch review and readiness assessment, but code and prose changes in documentation were written by me, paraphrasing the changes in Roberts original. I take full responsibility for the patch's contents.\n\nSome notes:\n1. \nI moved the DB element in `p.adjust.methods` to the end, which reduces the surface of possible breakage (positive int indexes into the vector will be the same for all previously present methods), though it doesn't remove it completely (length on, negative indexing into the vector will still give different results).\n\n2.\nThe `pairwise.*` family of functions capture the method for inclusion in the return object *before* the bonferroni -> BD alias is applied, so the result objects wil be identical to previous when method is set specifically to bonferroni; this is more backwards compatible but somewhat weakens the \"Dunn-Bonferroni is the canonical way to refer to [that method]\" claim. I think this is a reasonable tradeoff given R's focus on backwards compat, but its worth noting.\n\nI got a clean result for a manual run of R CMD check for the patch (with respect to this, my cairo is broken which causes a grid failure...).",
            "bug_id": 17845,
            "attachment_id": 4140,
            "time": "2026-11-21T23:21:17Z",
            "creator": "gabembecker",
            "is_markdown": true
          }
        ]
      }
    },
